==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*þunkijaną}}. Cognate with {{cog|osx|thunkian}}, {{cog|goh|dunken}}, {{cog|non|þykkja}}, {{cog|got|𐌸𐌿𐌲𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|þynċan}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|þynċan}}

# {{lb|ang|impersonal}} to [[seem]] (''+ dative'')
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=m2hVAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA308 "The Saints Alexander, Eventius, and Theodosius"]
#*: {{quote|ang|Þā cwæþ Aurēliānus, "Eom iċ hund '''ġeþūht'''?"|Then Aurelianus said, "Do I '''look like''' a dog?"}}
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=m2hVAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA382 "On the Festival of St. Peter the Apostle"]
#*: {{quote|ang|Sē enġel cwæþ, "Begyrd þē and sċō þē and fylġ mē." Petrus þā him fylġde, and '''þūhte''' him swelċe hit swefn wǣre.|The angel said, "Put on your belt and shoes and follow me." Peter followed him, and it '''felt''' to him like he was dreaming.}}
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=2xobVW92JLcC&pg=PA376 "On the Passion of the Apostles Peter and Paul"]
#*: {{quote|ang|Simōn bræġd his hīw ætforan þām cāsere, swā þæt hē wearþ fǣrlīċe '''ġeþūht''' cnapa, and eft hārwenġe.|Simon changed his appearance in front of the emperor, so that he suddenly '''looked like''' a boy, then like a bearded old man.}}
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], ''[[w:Old English Hexateuch|the Old English Hexateuch]]'', [https://books.google.com/books?id=QrZUAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA72 Genesis 29:20]
#*: {{quote|ang|Iacōb him hīersumode seofon ġēar for Rācēle, and hit '''þūhte''' him fēawa daga for þǣre lufe þe hē tō hire hæfde.|Jacob served him for seven years for Rachael, but it '''seemed''' to him like a few days because of the love that he had for her.}}
#* '''c. 990''', ''{{w|Wessex Gospels}}'', [https://archive.org/stream/dahalgangodspelo00thor#page/n63/mode/2up Matthew 23:27]
#*: {{quote|ang|Ġē sind ġelīċe hwītum byrġenum, þā '''þynċaþ''' mannum ūtan wlitiga, and hīe sind innan fulla dēadra bāna and ealre fȳlðe.|You're like whitewashed tombs, which '''look''' beautiful from the outside, while on the inside they're full of dead people's bones and all sorts of filth.}}
#* '''c. 990''', ''{{w|Wessex Gospels}}'', [https://archive.org/stream/dahalgangodspelo00thor#page/n211/mode/2up John 8:53]
#*: {{quote|ang|Hwæt '''þyncþ''' þē þæt þū sīe?|Who do you '''think''' you are? (Literally: Who does it '''seem''' to you that you are?)}}
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=eDdECgAAQBAJ&pg=PA161 "Preface to Genesis"]
#*: {{quote|ang|Þā unġelǣredan prēostas, ġif hīe hwæt lȳtles understandaþ of þām Lǣdenbōcum, þonne '''þyncþ''' him sōna þæt hīe mæġen mǣre lārēowas bēon.|If uneducated priests understand anything at all from the Latin texts, they immediately '''think''' [''lit.'' "it '''seems''' to them immediately"] they can be famous teachers.}}

